k (doesn't exist), and skills deployment is skipped (returns nil for skillsDir read). The agent deployment (if implemented as part of DeployRickCustomizations wrapper) would still run. \n\nBut if the implementation puts agent deployment INSIDE `deployRickCustomizations(skillsDir)` and it reads from skillsDir (not embedded), then... but no, the system prompt must be the wiki content from `templates/skills/` (embedded), not `.rick/skills/`. The `.rick/skills/` dir doesn't contain think.md/research.md/exporter.md (they're in `internal/prompt/templates/skills/`). So the implementation MUST read from embedded templates (internal/prompt) or embed its own copy in env.\n\nWait, let me double check `.rick/skills/` — does it contain think/research/exporter? From the earlier listing, `.rick/skills/` contains things like `check_mechanism_skill`, `command_registration_verification_skill`, etc. No think/research/exporter. And `templates/skills/` in internal/prompt contains `think.md`, `research.md`, `exporter.md`. So yes, agent system prompts come from embedded templates.\n\nSo the implementation will import `internal/prompt` (for `LoadCoreSkills` or `skillsFS`) or re-embed. Either way, cwd doesn't matter for agent content.\n\nFor my temp Go program, to keep things clean and avoid deploying rick-gates/skills into the temp agent dir (which could confuse checks), I'll run with cwd=repo_root. Actually deploying rick-gates/skills into the temp agent dir is harmless and actually tests the full DeployRickCustomizations. But the source `.rick/skills` has `rick-gates/helper.py` and skill dirs. Let me verify `.rick/skills/rick-gates` exists and has helper.py.\n\nFrom earlier `ls .rick/skills/`, there's `rick-gates` dir. And the task3 test deploys it. So running with cwd=repo_root will deploy rick-gates + skills into the temp agent dir. Fine, harmless.\n\nBut wait, there's a subtlety with `go run` and module. The temp program dir is under repo_root with a dot-prefix name like `.tmp_task9_x