{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"There's `pi --print -p` (non-interactive mode: process prompt and exit) and `--mode rpc`. The pi binary supports non-interactive mode. Could I use it to run `{action:\"list\"}`? The subagent tool is a builtin/extension tool. Running `pi -p \"list agents\" --mode json` might invoke the model... but that requires an API key and a model. Too heavy and unreliable.\n\nActually, wait — the subagent tool with `{action:\"list\"}` is a tool the LLM calls, not a CLI. There's no direct CLI to list agents. The research-report says `{action:\"list\"}` is the discovery entry via the subagent tool.\n\nGiven the test environment constraints, I'll use the \"equivalent mechanism\": verify agent files in the discovery directory with correct frontmatter names. This is the same data source pi's `{action:\"list\"}` reads (agent file discovery in `agents/**/*.md`). I'll document this in the script's docstring as the equivalent.\n\nNow, let me also verify: does the pi-subagents agent discovery actually read from `~/.pi/agent/agents/**/*.md` (user scope) → mapped to `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR/agents/**/*.md`? From research-report B2: \"User = `~/.pi/agent/agents/**/*.md`\". And rick's isolated mapping: \"user 级 = `~/.rick/pi/agent/agents/**/*.md`\". So `$RICK_PI_AGENT_DIR/agents/*.md` is correct.\n\nNow let me finalize the frontmatter expectations. What exactly will the `defaultContext` value be? The task says \"frontmatter 含 name/description/tools/defaultContext\". It doesn't specify value. I'll just check presence of the key.\n\nNow, an important consideration: should I check `description` is non-empty? The task says frontmatter contains `description`. I'll check presence and non-empty (since a description field with empty value would be useless). I'll check presence; optionally non-empty. Let me check presence + non-empty for description (to be meaningful).\n\nLet me also reconsider: the `tools` field. For think, task says `tools: read, grep, find, ls`. For research: `read, grep, find, ls, bash, web_search, fetch_content`. For exporter: `read, write, bash`. I'll assert exact match (normalizing whitespace/commas). I'll compare the normalized sorted list of tools.\n\nLet me normalize: split `fm['tools']` by comma, strip, sort → compare to sorted expected list. This is robust to ordering differences. But actually the task lists them in a specific order. I could check exact string too. To be safe against minor ordering, I'll compare as sorted sets. But then if the implementation has wrong tools (missing one), sorted-set comparison catches it. Good.\n\nActually, let me compare both: exact set equality via sorted lists, and also that the count matches. Sorted list equality covers both.\n\nNow let me also think: should the test script also check the Go source for `requiredRickAgents` being updated (i.e., CheckRickAgents lists the 3 agents)? The task goal doesn't explicitly require `requiredRickAgents` to change; key result 3 says \"验收断言用真实入口\". But the `CheckRickAgents` function currently has empty `requiredRickAgents`. Task9 might or might not update it. The task goal says \"此任务产出的是 env 职责 3（DeployRickCustomizations）中「agent」定制物的具体内容\" — focused on deployment, not the check. But key result 3 mentions discovery. The `CheckRickAgents` is职责4 (check), not 职责3. \n\nThe test method (the authoritative spec for my test) only mentions:\n1. Normal: 3 files exist + head -5 + discovery lists 3 names.\n2. Boundary: idempotent + overwrite.\n3. Exception: system prompt non-empty.\n\nSo I should focus on these. I could optionally add a structural source check that the deployment function exists and references the 3 agents, but the test method doesn't require it. I'll keep to the test method + key results, but add a light source check that `internal/env` source contains references to think/research/exporter agent deployment (to catch an implementation that only writes files without proper structure). Actually, that might be over-reach. Let me keep focused on behavioral assertions, which is the TDD-correct approach (test real behavior, not implementation details).\n\nActually, one more consideration: the test script should also verify the frontmatter has `defaultContext`. And the `rick-managed: true` marker. These are behavioral (file content) assertions.\n\nLet me now also decide: do I need to test `head -5 think.md 含 name: think`? The frontmatter is at the top, so `name: think` should be in the first 5 lines (after `---`). I'll read the first 5 lines and check `name: think` is among them. This is a direct translation of the test method's `head -5 think.md`.\n\nNow let me write the full script.\n\nLet me also think about timeouts and the `go run` overhead. Each `go run` compiles. With the toolchain already downloaded, it should be a few seconds each. I'll set timeout 240s for deploy runs.\n\nLet me also handle the case where `go run` might print \"go: downloading\" to stderr on first run — that's fine, it goes to stderr and doesn't affect my `out`/`err` parsing (I only check rc and 'deploy-ok' in stdout). Actually `go run` prints the program's stdout (\"deploy-ok\") to stdout, and any build messages to stderr. So `out` contains \"deploy-ok\". Good. But wait — could \"go: downloading\" appear in stdout? No, it's stderr. Fine.\n\nLet me now write the script.\n\nOne more thing: the `find_repo_root` fallback. The script is at `.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/tests/task9.py`, so up 5 levels from tests/ = repo root. Let me verify: \n- `__file__` = .../.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/tests/task9.py\n- dirname = .../.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/tests\n- up 5: tests(1) → doing(2) → job_35(3) → jobs(4) → .rick(5) → repo_root\n\nWait: dirname(5 times):\n1. dirname(tests) = doing\n2. dirname(doing) = job_35\n3. dirname(job_35) = jobs\n4. dirname(jobs) = .rick\n5. dirname(.rick) = repo_root ✓\n\nBut since `.git` is at repo_root, the `.git` probe will find it first. Good.\n\nNow let me write the script with all phases. I'll also add cleanup of temp dirs at the end (optional, but good hygiene). Actually temp dirs under /tmp are fine; I'll clean the Go program dir and build dirs.\n\nLet me carefully write parse_frontmatter to handle the closing `---` correctly. The frontmatter closes with a line that is exactly `---`. The system prompt body might also contain `---`? Looking at the skill files: think.md has `---` separator lines? Let me check. The skill files (templates/skills/think.md) — do they contain `---` lines? \n\nFrom my read of think.md (skill), I see `---` separators? Let me check: The skill file starts with `# skill:think(...)` then a paragraph, then `---`, then `## think pipeline`. Yes! The skill files contain `---` lines in the BODY (as markdown horizontal rules). So my frontmatter parser must only treat the SECOND `---` line as the closing delimiter (i.e., the first `---` after the opening `---`).\n\nMy parse_frontmatter finds the first line equal to `---` after index 0. Since frontmatter fields are `name:`, `description:`, etc. (no `---` among them), the first `---` after opening is the closing delimiter. Good. The body then contains subsequent `---` lines (markdown rules), but I don't care about those for parsing; I just take body = everything after the closing delimiter.\n\nBut wait — what if the description field's value is multi-line (YAML block) and contains `---`? Unlikely for this task; description will be a single line. I'll assume single-line frontmatter values.\n\nLet me verify the skill files don't have `---` immediately as line 1. The skill files start with `# skill:...`, not `---`. So the agent file = frontmatter (`---` + fields + `---`) + body (which starts with `# skill:...`). Good.\n\nNow, the awk in the test method: `awk '/^---$/{n++} n>=2 && /[^[:space:]]/{print}'`. This increments n on each `---` line and prints non-blank lines when n>=2 (i.e., after the 2nd `---`). This matches my body extraction (after 