{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"My parsing and discovery logic works correctly with the expected format. The test is well-formed.\n\nNow let me also verify that the test is robust: the \"覆盖语义（无标记）\" test pre-places a user file WITHOUT rick-managed, and expects it NOT to be overwritten. But my pre-placed file has frontmatter:\n```\n---\nname: think\ndescription: user custom agent\ntools: read\n---\nUSER CUSTOM BODY\n```\n\nAfter deploy, the implementation's overwrite logic checks if the existing file's frontmatter contains `rick-managed: true`. It doesn't → skip. So the file stays as `user_content`. My assertion `after_content == user_content` — but there's a subtlety: what if the implementation writes a trailing newline or normalizes? Since it skips writing entirely, the file content should be byte-identical to what I wrote. My `write_file` writes the string without a trailing newline. `read_text` returns exactly that. So `after_content == user_content` should hold. Good.\n\nActually wait, there's a subtle issue: the implementation might check for `rick-managed` marker using a different approach (e.g., substring search for `rick-managed` in the whole file, not just frontmatter). My user file has no `rick-managed` anywhere, so either way it's skipped. Good.\n\nFor the \"有标记\" test, the stale file has `rick-managed: true` in frontmatter. The implementation should overwrite it. After overwrite, content should no longer contain \"STALE BODY\" and should contain \"skill:research\". Good.\n\nBut one concern: my stale file has `tools: read` (single tool). If the implementation's overwrite logic is content-comparison based (i.e., \"rick 自有的占位/旧版内容必须按内容比对覆盖\"), it might compare and overwrite regardless. Either way, since it has the marker, it should be overwritten. My assertion checks it changed. Good.\n\nNow, let me also double check: is there any risk that the implementation writes the agent files to a DIFFERENT path than `$RICK_PI_AGENT_DIR/agents/`? The task says `runtime.AgentDir()/agents/`. `runtime.AgentDir()` respects `RICK_PI_AGENT_DIR` env var. So `$RICK_PI_AGENT_DIR/agents/`. My test uses `os.path.join(agent_dir, 'agents', ...)`. Correct.\n\nAlso, the `runtime.AgentDir()` reads env var `RICK_PI_AGENT_DIR`. My temp Go program inherits env via `os.environ.copy()` + set. Good.\n\nNow, let me consider whether the test should also verify `HOME` isolation. The task says \"尊重 RICK_PI_AGENT_DIR/HOME 隔离\". I set both RICK_PI_AGENT_DIR and HOME to temp dirs. The agent deployment uses `runtime.AgentDir()` (RICK_PI_AGENT_DIR), not HOME directly. But setting HOME to temp ensures no accidental writes to real home. Good.\n\nI think the test is complete and correct. Let me do a final review of the script for any issues:\n\n1. The `find_repo_root` fallback: correct.\n2. `run` handles FileNotFoundError and TimeoutExpired: correct.\n3. `write_deploy_program` uses dot-prefix: correct.\n4. `run_deploy` sets env and runs `go run . <target>`: correct.\n5. main flow: all three test method phases + structural go test.\n\nOne thing: In the normal path, if `rc != 0`, I skip the frontmatter checks AND the idempotent check (which is nested inside the `else`). That's fine — if deploy fails, we already have the error.\n\nBut wait, the idempotent check is nested inside `else` of normal path. If normal path deploy fails (rc != 0), we skip idempotent. That's acceptable since the primary failure is already reported. But it means the idempotent check won't run independently. Given the test method's boundary depends on \"已注册一次\" (which requires normal path success), this nesting is logically correct.\n\nHowever, there's a subtle issue: the overwrite semantics tests (ow_dir, mk_dir) and exception phase run INDEPENDENTLY of normal path (they're outside the else). Good — they run even if normal path fails. This is fine and provides more signal.\n\nActually, wait — there's a potential concern: if the normal path deploy fails because the temp Go program can't compile (e.g., env package has a compile error), then ALL deploy runs (ow, mk, ex) will also fail with the same error, producing redundant errors. That's acceptable — it's informative.\n\n6. The go test at the end runs `./internal/env/...`. Good.\n\nLet me also verify the script is executable-friendly (has shebang) and can be run directly. The task says \"添加 shebang: #!/usr/bin/env python3\". I have it. But the file mode — task3.py etc. have -rwxrwxr-x. My write created it with default mode (probably 0644). The task says \"脚本应该可以直接运行: python3 <path>\". Running via `python3` doesn't need +x. But to match convention and the \"可执行性\" requirement, I could chmod +x. The other task files are executable. Let me chmod +x to be safe.\n\nLet me also re-read the requirement: \"可执行性: 添加 shebang ... 脚本应该可以直接运行: python3 ...\". So `python3 <path>` is the canonical run. But adding +x is nice. Let me chmod +x."}}
